\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The cross-method matrix (pre-v1: fitted on 36 contexts and scored before the evaluation fixes of \cref  {app:protocol}; to be re-scored after the refit): medians over 22 depth-stratified targets, every score on held-out contexts; weighted-circuit rows apply their scaling coefficients (the $\alpha $ rows of \cref  {tab:semantics}). ``pass'' counts targets with faithfulness under both zero and mean ablation in $[0.8, 1.25]$ and $\phi _{\mathrm  {sup}}> 0.9$, a provisional rule until the pre-stated criterion is fixed; one target's mean-ablation denominator is vacuous and excluded. Top-$n$ rows truncate each attribution ranking to the target's weighted-circuit size; the hierarchical-attribution row replicates \citet  {ge2024automatically} on our sites with its threshold bisected to the same size.}}{9}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:matrix}{{2}{9}{The cross-method matrix (pre-v1: fitted on 36 contexts and scored before the evaluation fixes of \cref {app:protocol}; to be re-scored after the refit): medians over 22 depth-stratified targets, every score on held-out contexts; weighted-circuit rows apply their scaling coefficients (the $\alpha $ rows of \cref {tab:semantics}). ``pass'' counts targets with faithfulness under both zero and mean ablation in $[0.8, 1.25]$ and $\phisup > 0.9$, a provisional rule until the pre-stated criterion is fixed; one target's mean-ablation denominator is vacuous and excluded. Top-$n$ rows truncate each attribution ranking to the target's weighted-circuit size; the hierarchical-attribution row replicates \citet {ge2024automatically} on our sites with its threshold bisected to the same size}{table.2}{}}
